 Hayley Atwell and Tom Hiddleston Are Having a 'Merry War' on Broadway in Much Ado About Nothing 
 The two stars of Marvel's Cinematic Universe are bringing Shakespeare’s battle of wits to the Winter Garden Theatre.
 November 12, 2026 
 By Jeffrey Vizcaíno 
 Hayley Atwell and Tom Hiddleston
 (Heather Gershonowitz)
 There is already a small skirmish underway. Hayley Atwell and Tom Hiddleston are sitting together ahead of their Broadway engagement of Shakespeare’s Much Ado About Nothing , which has transferred to New York after its West End run, and the subject at hand is one of the most coveted pieces of real estate in the Winter Garden Theatre: Dressing Room 1. The room has had illustrious inhabitants ( Angela Lansbury , Chita Rivera ). Before this interview Atwell and Hiddleston were unaware of its legacy…until now. This is, naturally, enough information to begin a war.
 “Very interesting you should bring that up,” Atwell comments, suspiciously. “Because we couldn't see the dressing rooms today because they were being ‘renovated,’” she says making air quotes. She slyly shifts her eyes toward Hiddleston. “Under construction,” he adds smiling.
 “Yes, under construction,” Atwell repeats. “But now I'm like, is that some sort of—is Tom renovating them behind my back? Claim them as his own? I don't know. I think it's a...coin toss?” Hiddleston laughs. Hiddleston, listening to Atwell, supplies the Shakespearean diagnosis. “This is a merry war you’ve started here,” he says. “There's a skirmish of wit now about Dressing Room 1.”
 Atwell and Hiddleston, best known to Marvel Cinematic Universe audiences as Peggy Carter/Captain Carter and Loki, respectively, star as sparring lovers Beatrice and Benedick in Tony Award-winning director Jamie Lloyd ’s colorful production Shakespeare’s Much Ado About Nothing . Following its celebrated 2025 run at London’s Theatre Royal Drury Lane, the production has arrived at Broadway’s Winter Garden Theatre for a 10-week engagement, marking Atwell’s Broadway debut and Hiddleston’s return. Lloyd, whose recent Broadway revivals include Sunset Boulevard  and Waiting for Godot , will also bring his acclaimed West End revival of Andrew Lloyd Webber and Tim Rice’s Evita to Broadway this spring, starring Golden Globe winner Rachel Zegler.
 But back to Dressing Room 1. It is difficult to imagine a more appropriate opening gambit for Much Ado . In Shakespeare’s comedy, Beatrice and Benedick conduct their own long-running hostilities through verbal combat: They are lovers who do not yet know they are lovers, hiding behind jokes, insults, intellectual agility and the sheer pleasure of trying to outmaneuver each other.
 Hiddleston calls the phrase Shakespeare gives them, “a skirmish of wit,” the key to understanding the relationship. “I do think that that word wit is the key,” he begins, describing wit as “a construction of playful and competitive wordplay.” He compares their exchanges to “a game of tennis,” a contest of “one-upmanship,” but stresses that the point is pleasure. “Wit is supposed to delight both the participants and the spectators,” he says, “and that's what it does.”
 The distinction matters. Beatrice and Benedick are not simply mean to each other. Their hostility has rhythm, intelligence, mutual recognition. They are good at this. Perhaps too good. They have developed an entire language in which intimacy can masquerade as combat. “The play is about two very charismatic, very sophisticated people who have cocooned themselves inside their intellectual brilliance, but they don't know how to be vulnerable,” Hiddleston says.
 He continues: “The wisdom in the play is that they both have to set down the defense of their wit to be courageous enough, brave enough to accept themselves and accept each other, and love. And that's what I love about it.” 
 Atwell and Hiddleston have something useful for this particular Shakespearean exercise: They already know how the other person moves. Their first meeting, as they remember it, took place nearly 25 years ago, when they were young and auditioning for the Royal Academy of Dramatic Art (RADA). Atwell remembers Hiddleston as “a very gentleman, charming, naturally talented” person with “this fantastic mop of curly hair.” She pauses to clarify that she does not mean “mop” in the sense of something in need of cleaning. “It was dense,” she says. “Yeah, all these, like, very dense practical curls. Gorgeous.” Hiddleston is happy to accept the assessment. “You can clean the floor with my hair,” he says.
 His memory of the encounter is quieter. The audition, he recalls, was specifically intended to test how the prospective students worked with one another. “They'd said to us at the outset, 'We all know you can act, but we want to see you act with each other.'” He remembers Atwell’s “incredible poise and stillness,” qualities he says he did not possess at the time. He also remembers being struck by the possibility of what she might become. “I do remember thinking, this woman will be a great actress.”
 There is something almost perfectly Shakespearean about the symmetry of their recollections. One remembers the hair; the other remembers the stillness. One remembers a young man with extraordinary curls; the other remembers a young woman whose command of herself suggested something not yet fully visible. Now, years later, they are playing two characters who spend much of Much Ado pretending that they do not see what is plainly in front of them. That history becomes useful in the rehearsal room because the antagonism between Beatrice and Benedick has to be dangerous enough to feel real and safe enough to remain playful.
 Hayley Atwell and Tom Hiddleston
 (Heather Gershonowitz)
 The distinction between combat and play is fundamental for Atwell. “I suppose that that snarkiness is actually in the spirit of play, and so it's never mean-spirited or competitive,” she says. “You're wanting to create the scene that comes the most alive. Where we're both sort of playing a tennis match together and enjoying each other's moves.” The trust between them makes it possible to push harder. “You can go to deeper, darker places,” Atwell says, including the moments where the actors have to go after a character’s Achilles' heel. “You can go there because you know that it's to serve the scene, and that it's all done in the spirit of love.”
 And for Hiddleston, the word is simpler. “It's just trust, freedom, and permission between us.” That trust is also what makes returning to the production different from simply repeating it, especially after a full run in London. 
 Atwell puts it practically: “Doing a play is a living, breathing thing. It’s different every night because the audience is different every night. There is something in the air that never—you can't repeat it.” The text may be familiar, but Broadway offers new conditions. “The text is in our bones,” Atwell says, “and the relationship dynamics we create is in our bones now.” But “it’s also a different time and it's a different space and it's a different demographic and it's a different audience,” she says. “And discovering it fresh and new from that perspective, that becomes the joy and the challenge of it, and the thing that keeps us very present as we discover it.”
 For Hiddleston, the source material itself makes that process practically inexhaustible. “It's a masterpiece,” he says. “I really believe it.” He puts Much Ado in the company of Shakespeare’s greatest tragedies, describing it as a play of “depth and dimension” that is “endlessly rediscoverable.” The comedy, he says, contains an absurdly durable mechanism. “I used to call it a 400-year-old roller coaster,” he says grinning. “This has been funny for 400 years and it will be funny for the next 400 years, it's just funny." But Hiddleston is equally interested in the turns that make the play something more than a machine for laughs. He calls it “really redemptive” and says it has “an incredible, generous warmth in its spirit,” while also emphasizing that it is complex. “It's not superficial or reductive or saccharine,” he adds. “It takes a turn at a certain point.” 
 For Atwell, returning to the play means seeing how far she can push within the boundaries Shakespeare has created. “How brave can you be? And that's the question. There's such a—for me, such an exciting and creative one,” she says. “How can I take this further still within the world that exists here on the page?” The answer requires bravery. “You have to discover things,” she exclaims confidentially. “You have to be brave, and you have to be open to a moment of going, ‘Oh, maybe I don't know anything,’ or maybe, I don't know what I don't know." Hiddleston chimes in with this question: “And what can it reveal about who you're playing?” 
 Atwell says yes. And then, because this is Much Ado  à la Jamie Lloyd,  she adds another possibility. “And what can it reveal about my natural twerking skills?”
 Hayley Atwell, Tom Hiddleston, and company of Much Ado About Nothing 
 (Marc Brenner)
 That curiosity is part of the pleasure of arriving in New York for Atwell. She has watched Broadway as an audience member, and now she gets to experience it from the other side of the footlights. “I've always felt the energy in New York and the energy of a Broadway show is deeply invigorating for me, and contagious, and energizing,” she explains. At the time of the interview, she has already gone to see The Rocky Horror Show ; she was seeing Oh, Mary! that evening. “There’s a sense that there's a real conversation happening between stage and audience,” she says. That exchange becomes particularly meaningful when the production arrives carrying something familiar into an unfamiliar place. Atwell describes bringing a show she has loved so much with people she cares deeply for to a new audience as having its own “invigorating, exciting charm.” 
 Hiddleston describes Broadway in similarly communal terms. He says he finds the community of Broadway moving, and speaks of feeling invited and included in a rich and wonderful tradition of the city: “You just feel folded into the fabric of the city in a different way."
 Hiddleston recalls one night, after performing in Betrayal (also directed by Lloyd) at the Jacobs Theatre in 2019, when he was making his way home amid the crowds and traffic, when a police officer recognized him. “Hey, Loki! I got your back, man,” the officer told him. Hiddleston laughs at the memory, but the story fits his larger description of Broadway as an extended family. “It's just a love in,” he says. “And in the chill of like a November night? It's gorgeous.” 
 That idea of theatre as community eventually brings the conversation back to the audience. Atwell and Hiddleston know that some people may arrive because they already know them from their screen work. They do not see that as a problem to overcome. “We talked about that in rehearsal,” Atwell says. “There will be people who have come to see it because they love Marvel and they love the characters, and to embrace that.” The point, for her, is not to insist that an audience arrive with the correct credentials or the proper relationship to Shakespeare. “We're actually like, this…is part of the story in our production here,” she says. “And that's also the spirit of the play. Which is generous.” 
 That possibility is the entire point in Hiddleston’s opinion. “If they come and they take away the magic of the theatre, that's our job done.” He remembers being young and encountering theatre as something that opened up thoughts and feelings and worlds and ideas he might never otherwise have encountered. The live experience, he says, has a special power because of its immediacy. “You're just more present,” he says. “It’s almost at a cellular level, you never forget a piece of theatre that you've seen.” His description becomes less about Shakespeare specifically and more about a peculiar social miracle. “You're all in the same room, experiencing the same thing at the same time,” he says. “And if you arrive as strangers and you leave as a group with a shared understanding. Maybe you cried together, or you laughed together, or you thrilled together at the same thing. That makes you feel connected to other human beings, and it makes you feel less alone in the world.” 
 Hayley Atwell and Tom Hiddleston
 (Heather Gershonowitz)
 This is a surprisingly tender destination for a conversation that began with two actors trying to determine who gets the closest dressing room to the stage. But perhaps it is also the destination Shakespeare has been quietly arranging all along. Beatrice and Benedick begin with the pleasure of the skirmish. They are dazzling because they know how to keep each other at bay. Their words are shields, but they are also invitations. Each one keeps returning to the game because each has finally found someone capable of playing it. There is the tennis match. The one-upmanship. The “400-year-old roller coaster.” The “merry war.” The possibility of new dance moves. The possibility of twerking. And the possibility that after a record-breaking West End run and another rehearsal room and another city, there is still something neither actor knows yet about the characters they are playing. “How can I become a little bit more conscious about this blind spot I have about something else that can be done here or explored?” Atwell asks. It is the question of an actor returning to a role, but it is also the question that keeps a classic alive. The words have been waiting for centuries. The audience changes. The room changes. The actors change.
 And now, somewhere in the Winter Garden, with the renovations finished and the dressing-room battle finally settled, Beatrice and Benedick are back to the more important business of fighting. Or flirting. Or, as Shakespeare might prefer, doing both at once.
